### What is Stream.iterate?

`Stream.iterate` creates a stream by starting with a seed value and repeatedly applying a function to produce the next value. The element sequence is `seed, f(seed), f(f(seed)), ...`. Because the function keeps producing a new value from the last one, the stream has no natural end.

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class IterateBasics {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // seed = 0, each next = previous + 2  ->  0, 2, 4, 6, ...
        Stream.iterate(0, n -> n + 2)
                .limit(5)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
0
2
4
6
8
```

### How do you generate twelve monthly installment due dates?

The seed is the loan start date, and the step function adds one month. `limit(12)` stops after twelve dates.

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class DueDateSchedule {
    public List<LocalDate> nextTwelveDueDates(LocalDate start) {
        return Stream.iterate(start, d -> d.plusMonths(1))
                .limit(12)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate start = LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 15);
        new DueDateSchedule().nextTwelveDueDates(start)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
2024-01-15
2024-02-15
2024-03-15
2024-04-15
2024-05-15
2024-06-15
2024-07-15
2024-08-15
2024-09-15
2024-10-15
2024-11-15
2024-12-15
```

### Why is Stream.iterate infinite?

The two-argument form has no stopping condition built in. The function always produces a next value from the previous one, so the sequence continues forever. This is deliberate: it lets you describe an endless process and decide separately how much of it you need.

```java
// WRONG: no bound, this runs until memory or time is exhausted
Stream.iterate(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 15), d -> d.plusMonths(1))
        .forEach(System.out::println); // never terminates
```

```java
// CORRECT: limit(12) tells the stream how many elements to take
Stream.iterate(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 15), d -> d.plusMonths(1))
        .limit(12)
        .forEach(System.out::println); // stops after 12
```

The wrong version hangs because nothing tells it to stop. The correct version short-circuits at twelve.

### How does limit bound an infinite stream?

`limit(n)` is a short-circuiting intermediate operation. It allows at most `n` elements to pass downstream and then signals the source to stop producing. Because streams are lazy, the infinite source only ever computes those `n` values.

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class LazyProof {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stream.iterate(1, n -> {
                    System.out.println("computing next after " + n);
                    return n + 1;
                })
                .limit(3)
                .forEach(v -> System.out.println("got " + v));
    }
}
```

```text
got 1
computing next after 1
got 2
computing next after 2
got 3
```

Notice the step function runs only twice, not infinitely, because `limit(3)` stops the source after three elements.

### How does the three-argument iterate differ?

Java 9 added `Stream.iterate(seed, hasNext, next)`, which includes a predicate that stops the stream when it returns false. This self-terminating form needs no `limit`.

| Form | Signature | Termination |
|------|-----------|-------------|
| Two-argument | `iterate(seed, next)` | infinite, needs `limit` |
| Three-argument | `iterate(seed, hasNext, next)` | stops when `hasNext` is false |

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class SelfTerminating {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate start = LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 15);
        LocalDate end = LocalDate.of(2024, 6, 1);

        // Stops automatically when the date reaches end; no limit needed.
        Stream.iterate(start, d -> d.isBefore(end), d -> d.plusMonths(1))
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
2024-01-15
2024-02-15
2024-03-15
2024-04-15
2024-05-15
```

The three-argument form reads like a classic for-loop: seed, condition, step.

### How do you generate a running balance schedule?

A running balance also depends on the previous value, so `iterate` fits well. Each step subtracts the monthly principal from the previous balance. Money uses `BigDecimal`.

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class BalanceSchedule {
    public List<BigDecimal> runningBalance(BigDecimal principal,
                                           BigDecimal monthlyPrincipal,
                                           int months) {
        return Stream.iterate(principal, b -> b.subtract(monthlyPrincipal))
                .limit(months)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BalanceSchedule s = new BalanceSchedule();
        s.runningBalance(new BigDecimal("120000"), new BigDecimal("10000"), 6)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
120000
110000
100000
90000
80000
70000
```

### What are common mistakes with Stream.iterate?

The classic mistake is forgetting `limit` on the two-argument form, which hangs the program. Another is putting `limit` after an operation that must see every element, like `sorted`, which still consumes the infinite stream.

```java
// WRONG: sorted() needs all elements, so it never reaches limit
Stream.iterate(1, n -> n + 1)
        .sorted()        // tries to read the whole infinite stream
        .limit(10)
        .forEach(System.out::println); // hangs
```

```java
// CORRECT: limit before any operation that would consume everything
Stream.iterate(1, n -> n + 1)
        .limit(10)
        .sorted()
        .forEach(System.out::println);
```

Place `limit` as early as possible so the stream is bounded before any full-consumption operation runs.

### What are the edge cases?

A `limit(0)` yields an empty stream, and a predicate that is false for the seed in the three-argument form yields nothing at all.

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long zero = Stream.iterate(1, n -> n + 1).limit(0).count();
        long none = Stream.iterate(1, n -> n < 1, n -> n + 1).count();
        System.out.println("limit(0) count = " + zero);
        System.out.println("false-from-start count = " + none);
    }
}
```

```text
limit(0) count = 0
false-from-start count = 0
```

### How does iterate compare with generate?

`Stream.iterate` produces each value from the previous one, so the sequence is dependent and ordered. `Stream.generate` calls a supplier that ignores the previous value, so each element is independent.

| Aspect | Stream.iterate | Stream.generate |
|--------|----------------|------------------|
| Depends on previous | yes | no |
| Needs a seed | yes | no |
| Ordered | yes | unordered |
| Typical use | dates, balances, counters | random IDs, constants |
| Bounding | `limit` or 3-arg predicate | `limit` |

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class IterateVsGenerate {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("iterate (dependent):");
        Stream.iterate(1, n -> n * 2).limit(4).forEach(System.out::println);

        System.out.println("generate (independent):");
        Stream.generate(() -> ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(100))
                .limit(3)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
iterate (dependent):
1
2
4
8
generate (independent):
42
7
91
```

### What about parallelism?

Because each value depends on the previous one, `iterate` cannot be split cleanly across threads, so `parallel()` gives no benefit and can even slow it down. Keep iterate sequential.

```java
// Parallel on iterate does not help: each value needs the one before it.
Stream.iterate(0, n -> n + 1)
        .limit(1_000)
        .parallel()      // no real speedup for a dependent sequence
        .forEach(n -> {});
```

For independent work that parallelizes, prefer `IntStream.range` which splits into chunks easily.

### When should you use Stream.iterate?

Use `iterate` when each element is derived from the previous one: calendar schedules, amortization balances, Fibonacci numbers, exponential backoff delays, or page offsets. Use the three-argument form when there is a natural stopping condition, and the two-argument form with `limit` when you want a fixed count.

```java
package com.bank.loan;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class BackoffDelays {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Exponential retry backoff: 100, 200, 400, 800, 1600 ms
        Stream.iterate(100, d -> d * 2)
                .limit(5)
                .forEach(ms -> System.out.println("retry after " + ms + " ms"));
    }
}
```

```text
retry after 100 ms
retry after 200 ms
retry after 400 ms
retry after 800 ms
retry after 1600 ms
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers ask why the two-argument form is infinite, how `limit` short-circuits a lazy stream, the difference between the two-argument and three-argument forms, why `iterate` does not parallelize, and the difference between `iterate` and `generate`. A strong answer ties all of these to laziness: the source is endless, but a terminal operation only ever pulls as many values as it needs.
